  "live_records": {
    "q-34319": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34319",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+9{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+25{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,}=2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
