    "q-64732": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64732",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{9})}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$",
