    "q-132107": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132107",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}))}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{16})}=16.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
