    "q-64738": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64738",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8}{25})}=\\frac{25}{8}.\n$$",
