    "q-132112": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132112",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $r(\\pi )$ if the curve and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $r(\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
