    "q-97706": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97706",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is \n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟩.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,1⟩,$ since the point $P(1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(1+4{t}^{2})^{3}}},\\,-\\frac{4t}{\\sqrt{(1+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(1+4(0)^{2})^{3}}},\\,-\\frac{4(0)}{\\sqrt{(1+4(0)^{2})^{3}}}⟩=⟨2,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}⟨2,0⟩=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,0⟩=⟨\\frac{3}{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
