    "q-64839": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64839",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1)$ where $t=0$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩\\,.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,1⟩,$ since the point $P(0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖$: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2}+1)^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2}+1)^{3}}}⟩=⟨0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨0,1⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,1⟩+1⟨0,1⟩=⟨0,2⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
