      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=4$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\sqrt{t-3},t-1⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(4)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(4)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t-3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}},1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(4) & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(t-3)^{3}}},0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(4)$ and ${r}^{″}(4)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(4) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(4){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=5\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
