    "q-132119": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132119",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=2$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(2),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(2) & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(2)$ and ${r}^{″}(2)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(2) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(2){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}})}^{3}}{(\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{27}{16\\sqrt{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
