    "q-98717": {
      "dom_id": "step-q98717",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨0,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨-4,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-16\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{4}^{3}}{\\,16\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
